<p>Improving your Core Web Vitals from "poor" to "good" delivers an average 25 percent increase in conversion rates, a 35 percent reduction in bounce rates, and a 30 percent improvement in revenue per visitor. For a contractor generating $40,000 per month in website-driven revenue, that is an extra $10,000 per month from technical fixes alone.</p>

<h2>Critical Mistake #2: Hiding Your Phone Number and Making Contact Difficult</h2>

<p>The second most expensive contractor website mistake is making it hard for ready-to-buy visitors to actually contact you. This seems obvious, yet we audit dozens of contractor sites every month where the phone number is buried on a separate contact page, the contact form has twelve fields, or the only call-to-action says "Learn More" instead of "Call Now."</p>

<p>Homeowners do not browse contractor websites for entertainment. They have a problem that needs solving, often urgently. When they land on your site, they are in buying mode. Every extra click, every extra field, every extra second of searching for your contact information is an opportunity for them to give up and call someone else.</p>

<h3>The Cost of Weak Calls-to-Action</h3>

<p>A contractor site with passive CTAs like "Contact Us" or "Learn More" converts at roughly half the rate of a site with action-oriented CTAs like "Get a Free Estimate" or "Call for Emergency Service." The difference between "Submit" and "Get My Free Estimate" on a form button can increase completions by 20 to 30 percent. These are not marginal gains. On a site generating 1,000 visitors per month, improving CTA language from weak to strong can mean the difference between 10 leads and 20 leads.</p>

<p>The placement of your CTAs matters just as much as the wording. A phone number in the header converts better than one in the footer. A contact form embedded on every service page converts better than one isolated on a contact page. Multiple conversion points throughout a page convert better than a single CTA at the bottom. PipelineOn's research shows that adding three or more CTAs per page increases lead capture by approximately 37 percent.</p>

<h3>Form Fields: Every Extra Field Kills Conversions</h3>

<p>One of the most damaging contractor website mistakes is the overbuilt contact form. We regularly see forms asking for name, email, phone, address, city, state, zip code, preferred date, budget range, project description, square footage, how they heard about you, and whether they own or rent. Every additional field reduces form completions by roughly 10 percent. A twelve-field form might convert at 1 percent while a three-field form converts at 3 to 4 percent.</p>

<p>The optimal contractor contact form has exactly three to four fields: name, phone number, email, and a brief description of the problem. That is it. You do not need their mailing address to call them back. You do not need their budget range to schedule an estimate. You do not need to know how they found you to provide service. All of that information belongs in the sales conversation, not the initial contact form.</p>

