<p>The customer-centric versions lead with the problem, the solution, and the immediate benefit. They answer the homeowner's internal question: "Can this company fix my problem right now?" The company-centric versions answer a question nobody is asking: "How long has this company been in business?"</p>

<h3>The PAS Framework for Contractor Copy</h3>

<p>The Problem-Agitate-Solution framework is the most effective copywriting structure for contractor websites. Here is how it works on a service page:</p>

<p><strong>Problem:</strong> "A broken air conditioner in July is not just uncomfortable. It is dangerous for children, elderly family members, and pets. Temperatures inside your home can climb to 90 degrees or higher within hours."</p>

<p><strong>Agitate:</strong> "Waiting days for a repair appointment is not an option. Every hour without AC means another hour of heat, humidity, and stress. Meanwhile, some HVAC companies charge emergency fees that double your bill, or they send technicians who cannot diagnose the real problem."</p>

<p><strong>Solution:</strong> "Our emergency AC repair team is available 24/7 with no after-hours fees. We arrive within 2 hours, diagnose the issue with our 21-point inspection, and provide a flat-rate quote before any work begins. Most repairs are completed in under 90 minutes. Call now and get your home cool again today."</p>

<p>This structure works because it mirrors the homeowner's emotional journey. They have a problem. They are stressed about it. They need a solution they can trust. Your copy should walk them through that journey and position your company as the obvious answer.</p>

<h2>Common Mistake #9: Missing Schema Markup That Makes You Invisible to AI Search</h2>

<p>Schema markup is structured data that tells search engines exactly what your business is, what services you offer, where you are located, and how to contact you. Without it, Google, ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity have to guess. And when they guess, they often guess wrong or skip you entirely.</p>

<p>In 2026, schema markup has evolved from a nice-to-have technical optimization to a foundational requirement. AI search engines rely on structured data to generate answers. Voice assistants use schema to respond to "Hey Google, find an emergency plumber near me." Rich results in Google display your hours, pricing, and reviews directly in the search results, before users even click.</p>

<h3>The Schema Types Every Contractor Needs</h3>

<p>Here are the specific schema types that home service contractors should implement:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>LocalBusiness (or trade-specific subtype):</strong> Use the most specific type available. Plumber, Electrician, HVACBusiness, PestControlService, RoofingContractor, or GeneralContractor. Include name, address, phone, hours, geo-coordinates, and service area.</li>
<li><strong>Service schema:</strong> Mark up each individual service you offer with service type, description, provider, and area served.</li>
<li><strong>AggregateRating schema:</strong> Display your star rating and review count in search results. Must be based on real reviews visible on the page.</li>
<li><strong>FAQPage schema:</strong> Mark up the FAQ sections on your service pages to potentially earn FAQ rich snippets that expand your search listing.</li>
<li><strong>BreadcrumbList schema:</strong> Show your site hierarchy in search results, reinforcing topical authority and improving click-through rates.</li>
</ul>

<p>Websites with proper schema markup see 20 to 30 percent higher click-through rates than those without it. For a contractor ranking in position three for a high-value keyword, schema can be the difference between getting the click and being ignored.</p>

<h3>How to Implement Schema Without a Developer</h3>

<p>If you use WordPress, plugins like Yoast SEO, Rank Math, or Schema Pro can generate and deploy schema automatically. The key is to fill in every field accurately and ensure your schema data matches your Google Business Profile exactly. Inconsistent name, address, and phone information between your website schema, your Google Business Profile, and directory listings confuses search engines and hurts your local rankings.</p>

<p>Validate your schema using Google's Rich Results Test and the Schema.org Validator. Monitor the Enhancements section in Google Search Console for errors. Schema mistakes, like fake review markup or mismatched data, can trigger penalties.</p>

<h2>Common Mistake #10: Outdated Design That Signals an Outdated Business</h2>

<p>A website that looks like it was built in 2012 tells visitors that your business might operate like it is still 2012. Outdated design is not just an aesthetic problem. It is a trust problem. Homeowners wonder: If this company has not updated their website in ten years, what else have they not updated? Their equipment? Their training? Their insurance?</p>

<p>The specific design elements that signal outdatedness include: "Copyright 2018" footers, Flash animations, non-responsive layouts, cluttered sidebars, tiny text, clip art graphics, and auto-playing music. Any one of these can trigger an immediate back button click from a visitor who was otherwise interested.</p>

<h3>Modern Design Signals That Build Trust</h3>

<p>Conversely, modern design signals professionalism and attention to detail. Clean layouts with plenty of white space. Consistent branding across colors, fonts, and imagery. Fast load times. Mobile-responsive design. Professional photography. Clear typography. These elements work below conscious awareness to build confidence in your business.</p>

<p>The good news is that a modern contractor website does not need to be expensive or complex. A clean, simple design that prioritizes speed, clarity, and conversion will outperform a flashy, over-designed site every time. The pretty site that wins a design award and generates zero leads is a failure. The simple site that converts 10 percent of visitors into calls is a success.</p>

<h2>Common Mistake #11: No Analytics, No Tracking, No Idea What Is Working</h2>

<p>You cannot fix what you cannot measure. Yet a surprising number of contractor websites have no analytics installed, no conversion tracking set up, and no way to know which pages generate leads and which ones waste traffic. Without data, every decision is a guess. And guessing is expensive.</p>

<p>At minimum, every contractor website needs Google Analytics 4, Google Search Console, and conversion event tracking. You should know exactly how many visitors each page receives, how long they stay, where they drop off, and how many complete a contact form or click to call. You should know which traffic sources produce the highest-quality leads. You should know your cost per lead by channel.</p>

